{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Murray Hedgcock (born 23 February 1931) is an Australian cricket writer and journalist.","title":"Murray Hedgcock"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He was born in south Melbourne and grew up in various country towns in Victoria.","title":"Murray Hedgcock"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The test cricketer Bill Woodfull was the headmaster of one of his schools.","title":"Murray Hedgcock"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" After leaving school, he worked briefly in a bank before becoming a journalist.","title":"Murray Hedgcock"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" From 1966 until his retirement in 1991, he was posted to London.","title":"Murray Hedgcock"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" He wrote regularly for "The Australian", Wisden and "The Cricketer".","title":"Murray Hedgcock"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"John "Jack" Henry Webb Fingleton OBE (28 April 190822 November 1981) was an Australian cricketer who was trained as a journalist and became a political and cricket commentator after the end of his playing career.","title":"Jack Fingleton"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" A stubborn opening batsman known for his dour defensive approach, he scored five Test centuries, representing Australia in 18 Tests between 1932 and 1938.","title":"Jack Fingleton"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He was also known for his involvement in several cricket diplomacy incidents in his career, accused of leaking the infamous verbal exchange between Australian captain Bill Woodfull and English manager Plum Warner during the acrimonious Bodyline series, and later of causing sectarian tension within the team by leading a group of players of Irish Catholic descent in undermining the leadership of the Protestant Don Bradman.","title":"Jack Fingleton"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In retirement, Fingleton became a prominent political commentator in Canberra, with links to Australian prime ministers.","title":"Jack Fingleton"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The author of many cricket books, he is regarded as one of Australia's finest cricket writers, with a perceptive and occasionally sardonic style, marked by persistent criticisms of Bradman.","title":"Jack Fingleton"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Bill Ponsford
